  "hints": [
    "Model each word as a node, with an edge between words that differ in exactly one position. You want the shortest path.",
    "Breadth-first search from start finds the fewest steps, since every edge costs the same.",
    "Do not compare every pair of words. For the current word, try each position with each of the 26 letters and look the result up in a set, removing words from the set once they are queued."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function fewestSteps(start, goal, words) {\n  const unseen = new Set(words)\n  if (!unseen.has(goal)) return 0\n  unseen.delete(start)\n  let frontier = [start]\n  let length = 1\n  while (frontier.length) {\n    length++\n    const next = []\n    for (const word of frontier) {\n      for (let i = 0; i < word.length; i++) {\n        for (let code = 97; code <= 122; code++) {\n          const candidate = word.slice(0, i) + String.fromCharCode(code) + word.slice(i + 1)\n          if (!unseen.has(candidate)) continue\n          if (candidate === goal) return length\n          unseen.delete(candidate)\n          next.push(candidate)\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    frontier = next\n  }\n  return 0\n}\n",
    "py": "from string import ascii_lowercase\n\n\ndef fewest_steps(start, goal, words):\n    unseen = set(words)\n    if goal not in unseen:\n        return 0\n    unseen.discard(start)\n    frontier = [start]\n    length = 1\n    while frontier:\n        length += 1\n        nxt = []\n        for word in frontier:\n            for i in range(len(word)):\n                for ch in ascii_lowercase:\n                    candidate = word[:i] + ch + word[i + 1:]\n                    if candidate not in unseen:\n                        continue\n                    if candidate == goal:\n                        return length\n                    unseen.remove(candidate)\n                    nxt.append(candidate)\n        frontier = nxt\n    return 0\n",
    "explanation": "Breadth-first search explores chains level by level, so the first time goal is generated its chain is shortest. Neighbours are generated by trying all 26 letters at each position and checking a set, avoiding an all-pairs comparison. Deleting words from the set when they are queued ensures each word is expanded at most once."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(N · L² · 26)",
    "space": "O(N · L)",
    "notes": "N words of length L. Each word is expanded once and builds 26 · L candidates of length L. Bidirectional BFS often explores far fewer words in practice."
  },
  "followUps": [
